---
/**
 * src/pages/index.astro
 * =====================
 * Homepage — migrated from /index.html
 */
import BaseLayout from "@layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Banner from "@components/site/Banner.astro";
import ArticleCards from "@components/listings/ArticleCards.astro";
import PostList from "@components/listings/PostList.astro";
import TwoColumns from "@components/ui/TwoColumns.astro";
import FeatureSlider from "@components/ui/FeatureSlider.astro";
import Figure from "@components/article/Figure.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Home"
  description="This is the homepage"
  activeButton="Home-button"
>
  <Banner />

  <div class="content-grid">
    <header>
      <div class="topic">Homepage</div>
    </header>

    <section>
      <FeatureSlider
        slides={[
          {
            id: "chladni",
            title: "Chladni Patterns - The Wave Equation Model",
          },
          {
            id: "big-o",
            title: "Theoretical Runtime Analysis of an Algorithm ",
          },
        ]}
      >
        <Fragment slot="chladni">
          <TwoColumns>
            <div>
              <p>
                Take a solid metal plate (or any other rigid material) and
                sprinkle some particles on it. It can either be sand, salt, or
                even baking soda. Then, generate some vibration on the plate by
                using either a violin bow to excite the plate, or place it on a
                wave generator and set it to specific frequencies. What you will
                observe are the beautiful patterns generated as shown in the
                figure. This experiment sounds relatively simple, but as simple
                as it sounds, this experiment reveals some extremely complex and
                intensive underlying mathematical principles.
              </p>
              <p>
                Ok, so how does this work? Well, it turns out these are examples
                of standing waves in the two-dimensional space. In one
                dimension, a &ldquo;node&rdquo; in a
                <a
                  class="url"
                  href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standing_wave"
                  >standing wave</a
                >
                is a point along the wave where the amplitude is at minimum. Similarly,
                in two dimensions, there are &ldquo;nodal lines,&rdquo; which are
                static points or lines on the plate where the particles settle.
              </p>
              <a
                href="articles/the-quest-to-finding-chladni-patterns-1/"
                class="redirect-button column-center"
              >
                Read More
              </a>
            </div>
            <div>
              <Figure
                src="/media/Images/chladni-patterns-sbs.webp"
                alt="Chladni plate"
                width="75%"
              >
                Chladni Patterns on a Square Plate.
              </Figure>
            </div>
          </TwoColumns>
        </Fragment>

        <Fragment slot="big-o">
          <TwoColumns>
            <div>
              <p>
                Given two algorithms that perform the same task, what is the
                best way to compare their performance? Timing them is the
                obvious answer and also the wrong one: a runtime depends far
                more on the hardware it was measured on than on the algorithm
                itself, and it varies from run to run even on the same machine.
                What is wanted is a measure of how an algorithm scales as its
                input grows, independent of the machine underneath it.
              </p>
              <p>
                That measure is time complexity. Write f(n) for the worst-case
                cost of an algorithm on an input of size n, and the hardware
                becomes constant factors that the big-O notation then discards,
                leaving only the trend: constant, logarithmic, linear,
                quadratic, exponential, etc. From that definition come the
                properties of big O, big Omega, and big Theta, the Master
                Theorem, and the theoretical analysis of real algorithms.
              </p>
              <a
                href="articles/time-complexity-of-an-algorithm/"
                class="redirect-button column-center"
              >
                Read More
              </a>
            </div>
            <div>
              <Figure
                src="/articles/time-complexity-of-an-algorithm/p1_filter.webp"
                alt="Plot comparing the growth of common runtime functions"
                width="85%"
              >
                Two different implementations of maximum subarray.
              </Figure>
            </div>
          </TwoColumns>
        </Fragment>
      </FeatureSlider>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h1 class="home-title">Posts</h1>
      <PostList />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h1 class="home-title">Articles</h1>
      <ArticleCards showDetails={true} />
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* The "Read More" buttons under each featured article on this page. */
  .redirect-button {
    min-width: 80px;
    display: inline-block;
    position: relative;
    text-align: center;
    margin: 20px auto;
    background-size: 200%;
    border: 1px solid var(--next-border);
    border-radius: 5px;
    padding: 0.7rem 1rem;
    font-size: 18px;
    color: var(--next-text);
    font-weight: 500;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .redirect-button:hover {
    color: #2a7ae2;
  }
</style>
